In this study, we propose a Full-Window Augmented Reality System (FARS), an AR display system to improve passenger services (Fig. 1). FARS implemented an augmented reality screen in the rear passenger side window of the vehicle, allowing passengers to experience the AR system firsthand. To take into account the external environment, FARS is built in a real vehicle using a short-throw projector and a transparent display film, so that the entire side window of the passenger can be used as an AR display. We implemented the 3D Coordinates Augmented Reality (3DCAR) algorithm to provide passengers with AR content that takes into account their surroundings. 3DCAR augments the virtual information displayed on the window screen by taking into account the location of real-world buildings along the vehicle’s path, their distance from the vehicle, and the orientation of the building, allowing users to interact with their surroundings. To reflect the real-time position of the vehicle, a Real-Time Kinematic (RTK) (Wanninger 2004; Edwards et al. 1999) device was used instead of the commonly used Global Positioning System (GPS). RTK devices are used to display location-based AR content that contains information about the environment on top of the external environment visible through the window. The AR content is augmented on top of the surrounding buildings, and the location and information of the buildings can be viewed through the window. To validate our system, we conducted the user test with the following three hypotheses:
● H1: In-car system using RTK device will be able to display AR content in a more accurate location than using GPS device.

