In this study, we show that it is more effective to use RTK than GPS in FARS, an AR display system for passengers. Through user studies, we have empirically confirmed that FARS is effective in enhancing human spatial knowledge and improving the riding experience compared to SBD. To the best of our knowledge, our work is the first to compare passengers’ spatial knowledge in in-vehicle AR systems using real moving vehicles. This study aims to implement a novel infotainment system for non-driver passengers in a moving vehicle and provides detailed insights into the design of future in-vehicle AR systems that can enhance passengers’ spatial knowledge and improve their riding experience.
2  Related work
2.1  Infotainment systems for passengers
The automotive industry and researchers have been trying to improve the experience of non-driver passengers by introducing rear seat infotainment systems to create a unique ride experience (Berger et al. 2019). In particular, mobility services including public transportation such as taxis, buses, and airplanes have been increasingly using "seatback displays" (SBDs) - monitors mounted on the back of the front seats - to display information to passengers (Ng et al. 2021). Furthermore, these studies are becoming increasingly important as the development of autonomous vehicles will allow vehicles to be used as offices or mobile entertainment spaces when the driver becomes a passenger (Schartmüller et al. 2018).
HUDs were developed in the military in the late 1950 s to display flight-related information in a pilot’s field of vision, Prinzel III and Risser (2004) and began to be used in automobiles in the 1980 s to display information in a driver’s field of vision by projecting light onto a windshield or combiner screen, Weihrauch et al. (1989). HUDs are superior to head down displays (HDDs), such as center stack displays, in terms of safety and usability because they do not require the driver to look down to view navigation or vehicle information, and can reduce driver tension and stress (Beck et al. 2019). Therefore, there has been a lot of research on using

in-vehicle HUDs to display various information in the driver and passenger’s field of view (Park and Park 2019; Liu and Wen 2004; Beck et al. 2019). Liu and Wen (2004) reported that HUDs improved driver performance and reduced mental stress compared to HDDs. However, even with HUDs, the effect is not maximized unless the information is properly combined with real-world objects.
Augmented Reality (AR) HUDs have been researched to overcome these shortcomings of HUDs (Bauerfeind et al. 2019, 2022; Kim and Dey 2009; Lindemann et al. 2018; Bengler et al. 2015). Augmented Reality (AR) displays can accurately superimpose virtual information with associated real-world objects in the external environment while preserving perspective. The spatial proximity of real and virtual objects in an AR environment allows users to comprehend information quickly and easily. Rusch et al. (2013) reported that information presented by AR displays can be easily mapped to real-world traffic conditions, making them effective in providing users with event awareness of the external environment. Previous studies have shown that users require less mental effort to understand and interpret information when viewing an AR HUD compared to a HUD or HDD without AR information, which can reduce cognitive load (Pfannmüller et al. 2015; Kim and Dey 2009; Bengler et al. 2015). AR can increase drivers’ situational awareness, allowing them to better anticipate lane change behavior (Langlois and Soualmi 2016), and contribute to a positive impact on driving behavior by providing drivers with visual safety and a sense of relaxation (Okumura and Shinohara 2016; Calvi et al. 2020). In these services, AR technology has been used to enhance passengers’ travel experience and alleviate motion sickness (Elbanhawi et al. 2015; Berger et al. 2021). In addition, superimposing information related to external environmental landmarks on AR using AR HUD technology provides a positive experience for users (Wang et al. 2016, 2019). AR enables 3D visualization by projecting depth-conscious content in the real environment, which can improve users’ situational awareness and comfort. This is an important consideration for creating spatial navigation applications.
2.2  Windshield display
Conventional HUDs have a small display area (about 3-6 inches), which limits the information they can display, and they are spatially isolated from the external environment, requiring occupants to map the information on the screen to the actual external situation. Therefore, researchers have been working on Windshield Display (WSD), which extends the display area to the entire windshield (Sato et al. 2006; Charissis et al. 2013; Wu et al. 2009). WSDs allow drivers to use the windshield as an interaction surface or display,

