covering a large field of view (FOV) area (Häuslschmid et al. 2015). It can also increase driving safety (Gabbard et al. 2014) and trust (Wintersberger et al. 2018) because drivers do not have to change their gaze from the external environment to obtain information while driving. In addition, focusing on objects at different distances requires less effort between the real scene and virtual AR compared to HUDs (Häuslschmid et al. 2015). Schartmüller et al. (2018) conducted a user study and found a higher preference for WSD over HDD when performing work-related tasks. Furthermore, it is important to consider the location and perspective of the external environment when creating AR content (Takaki et al. 2011). Riegler et al. (2020) found that dynamically displaying AR content on WSD based on the location of the external environment improved task performance and reduced error rates.
WSD research is evolving into full-windshield HUD (FHUD) research, which extends the entire front of a vehicle’s windshield as a display area. Sato et al. (2006) developed a FHUD and proposed a new navigation system that allows the driver to see visual cues with little eye movement. The system was implemented by using a projector mounted on the roof of the vehicle and reflecting the projected image through a mirror. FHUD can help give drivers more information and reduce the frequency with which they look down (Charissis and Papanastasiou 2010). Other researchers have used driving simulators with a real vehicle parked in a simulated VR space, typically using a projection screen on the wall (Zhang et al. 2021; Fu et al. 2013; Gabbard et al. 2019; Bolton et al. 2015). For example, Zhang et al. (2021) implemented FHUDs on windshields and studied driver attention in different weather scenarios and found that FHUDs improved drivers’ visual attention allocation and helped them recognize signals more effectively. While these studies have shown that FHUDs improve driver performance and confirm the usefulness of the system, most of these studies have only tested drivers using in-cabin simulations.
There are also studies that have implemented FHUD in passenger seats using projectors and simulators (Wang et al. 2016, 2017, 2019). Wang et al. (2019) proposed an infotainment application for rear-seat passengers using a VR simulator in a Cave Augmented Virtual Environment (CAVE) room, and received a favorable evaluation of the system efficiency and positive user feedback. Their other studies (Wang et al. 2016, 2017) reported that gaming applications based on the environment can be used to engage passengers and reduce driver distraction. However, these studies implemented their systems using software simulations rather than real vehicles, so they were unable to validate their systems in a moving vehicle. They also failed to account for errors in the actual distance to objects outside the vehicle, which can

be caused by errors in location-based sensors, such as GPS, and the external environment, such as illumination.
2.3  Spatial knowledge
Navigation is a fundamental task in wayfinding and traveling to remember the location of a target object, to remember what is around it, or to navigate through space. Passengers in public transportation, such as buses and trains, need to recognize the location of the vehicle and explore the surrounding information to find their destination while traveling. Similarly, when getting off a mobility service, it is necessary to recognize the exact location of the destination and remember the surrounding information to reach the desired destination (path-finding). For example, you can find your destination by remembering the location and information about buildings, such as the city hall to the north and the pharmacy to the right of the city hall. Spatial knowledge plays an essential role in navigation and is used by humans to remember the location of objects and routes to find previously visited places (Burgess et al. 2001). Spatial knowledge enables the driver of a vehicle to navigate to a destination and passengers to understand their surroundings and find the places they want to go.
Spatial knowledge in navigation is related to the coordinates and information (e.g. names) of objects. This is why commercial navigation services show you the buildings around you and label them with their names. The convergence of virtuality and reality superimposes virtual information on real space, making it easier for users to perceive spatial information (Chu et al. 2017; Scarr et al. 2013). In particular, AR content can intuitively display information about an object in addition to the location of a visible object (e.g., a building, a landmark). Juan et al. (2014) developed a tool to measure spatial knowledge in AR and showed that it was able to assess children’s spatial knowledge. MunozMontoya et al. (2018) placed virtual AR objects in a realworld environment and measured users’ recall, and found that users remembered the locations of virtual objects better when using AR than when simply viewing photos of the environment. In addition, previous studies have shown that 3D AR content helps users acquire spatial abilities and increases their interest (Shakeri et al. 2023; Shaghaghian et al. 2024; Özçakır and Çakıroğlu 2022).
Many services and studies have used smartphones or AR devices to display information about buildings or landmarks in the environment based on location. Chu et al. (2017) proposed a mobile AR tool that guides users to destinations by augmenting virtual 2D icons with the real-world environment captured by the smartphone camera, and showed that the AR tool was significantly better than a flat map in terms of accuracy and task completion time. Rehman and Cao

