(2016) compared navigation systems using Google Glass, smartphones, and paper maps, and found that users perceived the AR content on Google Glass as the most accurate tool with the lowest workload and execution time. These studies also emphasize the importance of spatial knowledge, or how users perceive space through the combination of their surroundings and AR virtual objects.
In the past, metrics for studying spatial knowledge have included: i) object recall accuracy (e.g., how accurately a person remembers objects that appear on a path) (Buttussi and Chittaro 2023), and ii) object location accuracy (e.g., how accurately a person marks the location of an object on a map) (Buttussi and Chittaro 2023; Langbehn et al. 2018). These types of spatial knowledge are strongly interrelated, as people can often use one type of spatial knowledge to infer other types of spatial knowledge. On the other hand, each type of spatial knowledge can also be used independently, e.g., humans may not remember the exact location of a particular object, but they can easily remember the relationship between that object and another object.
A few researchers have proposed designs for interactive AR infotainment systems for rear-seat passengers that attach an LCD to the rear side window to display Points of Interest (POIs) such as restaurants, hotels, etc. Li et al. (2013); Rao et al. (2014). However, these studies are limited by the fact that they are prototype designs and do not take into account GPS systems, algorithms to link the vehicle location to the surrounding environment, etc. Bolton et al. (2015) found that a system using AR to highlight landmarks in a driving simulator could improve driver navigation performance by reducing driver response time and success rate, and significantly reducing workload. However, in the study, landmarks were simply displayed as boxes or arrows, with no information for the user to identify information about the landmark (e.g., the name of the building), and the system was not evaluated for its effect on spatial knowledge. Therefore, it would be meaningful to validate how effective an in-cabin AR system would be in terms of spatial knowledge.
2.4  RTK device
Most popular devices, such as smartphones and headsets, implement AR in a confined, slow-moving environment (Guarese and Maciel 2019). However, in fast-moving environments such as vehicles, it is difficult to place objects based on the exact position of the vehicle and the target. In particular, implementing AR systems on roads is very difficult due to the problem of accurately tracking the position and orientation of vehicles in real time (Gabbard et al. 2019). To augment virtual objects in a moving environment, localization is used to ensure that virtual objects are augmented to Points of Interest (POIs) defined in the Earth’s

coordinate system using the Global Positioning System (GPS), which determines the position and orientation of the vehicle in the global coordinate system (Scheibert 2020). However, despite a steady stream of research into the performance of GPS (Zangenehnejad and Gao 2021), it is difficult to measure precise locations because GPS typically exhibits an accuracy range of 1 to 10 ms (MacIntyre and Coelho 2000; Li et al. 2015).
To overcome the inaccuracies of GPS, a growing number of systems use Real-Time Kinematic (RTK) (Lenain et al. 2003; Ling et al. 2019). RTK uses carrier phase measurements of the signal in addition to information from the satellite signal and provides real-time corrections from a single reference station or interpolated virtual stations to provide accuracies down to the centimeter level (Wanninger 2004; Edwards et al. 1999). Previous study integrating RTK device with wearable AR device showed that RTK device have higher accuracy than GPS devices, and can achieve 1 cm accuracy in open spaces (De Pace and Kaufmann 2023). However, previous study verified that accuracy dramatically reduces in the proximity of obstacles or buildings. We need to evaluate whether the integration of an AR interface and RTK device is advantageous for vehicles traveling between buildings.
3  Full-window augmented reality system (FARS)
3.1  System architecture
FARS consists of an interface that allows rear-seat passengers to access external information in real-time via location-based sensors, as shown in Fig. 2. To implement the FARS in a real vehicle, the system was developed in a Mercedes-Benz C-Class. A custom transparent display AR film from Anyactive (AnyRactive) was applied to the rear side window. The film is produced by a combined vision control method that uses light blocking and light diffusion control techniques to reflect light from the projector, allowing AR content to be displayed on the external environment visible through the transparent glass window. A short-throw projector (AnyRactive) with a resolution of 1280 × 800 pixels, a brightness of 3,500 lumens, and a size of 198 × 30.8 × 21.6mm (W × H × D) was mounted on a tripod in the center of the rear seat, and AR content was projected onto the rear side window. The projection area displaying the AR content is 550mm wide and 400mm high. To obtain the precise position of the vehicle, we used the EM-TL30KL (EM Wave), which is a network RTK modem model that provides centimeter-level accuracy using the ZED-F9P (U-blox) module. The EM-TL30KL uses the

