To obtain the exact position of the vehicle, the latitude, longitude and altitude coordinates were received from the ZED-F9T device to the laptop using a program written in C#. To receive the packets, we used standard GGA (Global Positioning System Fix Data) packets with NMEA protocol from GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System) and NAV-HPPOSLLLH (High Precision Position Solution in Latitude, Longitude, and Height) packets with UBX protocol according to the instructions in the ZED-F9T manual provided by u-blox company (U-blox).
As shown in Fig. 3, the received position data is passed to the Unity client installed on the laptop. Since the RTK position data is represented in the coordinate system of the real world, and the information to be exposed as AR content is represented by the coordinates in the virtual world, it is necessary to convert the coordinate data of the building into the coordinate system and distance unit of the Unity client in order to map the geospatial information in the virtual space. Therefore, based on existing research (Bekele 2019), we went through the process of converting position data (latitude, longitude, altitude) into Unity coordinates.
The forward direction vector of the vehicle is the normalized vector from the previous frame position to the current frame position, and the camera viewing direction for AR content rendering is the normal vector of the forward direction vector facing the AR display.
Map editor To collect information about the environment, we built a map editor (Fig. 3) as a web application that allows users to enter the location and shape of buildings on the web. The user clicks to enter the vertices that make up the plane of the building on the 2D map displayed by the map editor. The coordinates are computed as latitude and longitude using the Google Maps API (Google Maps). The height and name of the building are entered and saved manually by the user. Once the coordinate data is entered, the map editor

