4.1 Measurements
Update distance We calculated the update distance to observe how quickly
the vehicle’s position was updated. The update distance evaluates the difference between the position data collected by each location-based sensor between frames and calculates the average value. Since the vehicle is constantly moving, a smaller update distance value means that the position data is changing at a faster rate.
Rendering frequency To measure how often the AR content projected onto the screen is rendered, we calculated the update rate of the AR objects per second for each of the GPS and RTK methods. To get the rendering frequency of the object, we measured how often the position of the vehicle (latitude, longitude) was updated. However, we avoided updating the position if the same value occurred repeatedly. While traversing the experimental path, we extracted the position values of the vehicle as raw data and calculated the frames per second (fps) based on whether the data changed or not. Positioning error For each GPS and RTK method, we collected data on whether the FARS system followed the correct path when following a given route. The correct route was defined by the center of the road within the defined path on Google Maps. Positioning error was calculated as the shortest distance from the collected location to the prescribed route. The lower the value, the more accurate the path.
