4.2 Procedure
This experiment was conducted under the same conditions with different positioning devices (RTK and GPS) to compare FARS performance. The experiments were conducted with a real FARS-equipped vehicle on a university campus in a controlled environment with few vehicles and pedestrians and no distractions such as traffic lights. Out of dozens of trials, we took only 20 valid trials each for GPS and RTK, excluding trials that were interrupted by other vehicles or people. The vehicle was driven by an experienced driver at a constant speed (M = 15.2 km/h, SD = 0.9) along a predetermined 435 m route.
4.3  Experimental results
The experiment involved calculating the average value of data collected over 20 repetitions of the entire experimental route without interference from other vehicles or pedestrians. The results confirmed the normal functioning of the 3DCAR algorithm across all trials. In particular, the accuracy of driving along the designated path inevitably influenced the positioning error results. However, the same experienced driver performed several practice runs, and the influence of the driver was reduced to some extent by analyzing the average of the total number of trials.
Update distance Figure 6 shows the result of plotting the vehicle’s actual location (latitude, longitude) on a map. Each axis is the relative coordinates in meters, with the horizontal axis showing east meters and the vertical axis showing north meters. In the case of RTK, the vehicle’s position changes in centimeters (M = 4.2 cm) and is plotted as points along the route, almost like a curve. On the other hand, when GPS is used, the position of the vehicle is changed in meters (M = 126.4 cm), and the gap between the obtained positions is large, which shows that the exact position is not obtained consistently. Rendering frequency Figure 7a shows the results of how often the AR object is rendered. In the RTK condition, the average rendering frequency was 29.57 fps (SD = 0.49), while in the GPS condition, the average was 1.71 fps (SD = 0.66). The experimental results showed that the RTK condition rendered the AR object 17.63 times faster than the GPS condition, resulting in a more natural movement of the object. Positioning error Figure 7b shows the positioning error of the vehicle in centimeters (cm) relative to the specified path. In the RTK condition, the positioning error averaged 7.53 cm (SD = 5.09), while in the GPS condition, it averaged 61.15 cm (SD = 48.04).

