4.4 Discussion Using RTK in FARS, the rendering frequency was 17.63 times faster than GPS. We found that when using GPS sensors, AR objects are rendered at a significantly lower rate, which can lead to stuttering of the AR object’s movement, making it difficult for users to determine their current location. As you can see in Figs. 6 and 7b, the update distance is 122.2 cm greater for the RTK condition than for the GPS condition, while the positioning error is 53.62 cm greater for the GPS condition than for the RTK condition, indicating that the position from GPS is relatively inaccurate. These results suggest that RTK is more suitable for implementing AR systems in moving vehicles. We conducted further

In Study 2, we conducted an experiment comparing FARS with the SeatBack Display (SBD) system commonly used in conventional infotainment systems to test the usability of the FARS system. In the FARS condition, we used the same system as in Study 1, and both conditions used RTK as the location-based sensor. For the comparison experiment, the SBD condition was implemented by changing the display type, as shown in Fig. 8, and a 12-inch surface tablet is mounted on the back of the front passenger seat so that the screen can be viewed by the passenger sitting in the back seat. In the SBD condition, the external environment around the building visible through the window was captured by a smartphone camera and displayed on the SBD screen in real time, similar to the previous studies that implemented an AR HDD (Rao et al. 2014; Shahriar and Kun 2018). AR objects were augmented on the building in the video for SBD in the same way as in FARS. The smartphone camera was placed on the rear side window at the same eye level as the participant, and the position of the smartphone was adjusted to avoid lens distortion and light reflection. The smartphone camera and tablet were wired together to ensure no delay in video transmission.
During the experiment, AR objects appeared on eight designated buildings, each labeled with the name of a different fruit. While traveling in a vehicle, participants had to remember as accurately as possible the name of the fruit and the location of the building to which the fruit name was attached, as shown on the display. Different fruit names were used in the FARS and SBD conditions. After reaching the destination, participants wrote the name of the fruit they remembered at that location on the paper map showing the experimental route and the location of the buildings. The order of the FARS and SBD conditions was counterbalanced.
5.2  Experimental evaluation
For quantitative measures, we measured two things in each condition (1) the number of fruit names in participants’ responses that actually appeared as the AR objects (Name accuracy), and (2) the number of correct matches between the location of the building and the fruit name (Matching accuracy).

