For the qualitative measures, participants completed four post-test questionnaires for each condition. The first three were traditional multidimensional assessment tools, namely the NASA-TLX (Hart and Staveland 1988) (Task Load Index), TAM (Davis 1993) (Technology Acceptance Model), and SUS (Brooke et al. 1996) (System Usability Scale). The NASA-TLX is a standard questionnaire for assessing user-perceived task difficulty and is often used to assess task performance and efficiency for cognitive load in a specific task. For example, it can be used as a tool to subjectively assess the mental workload of viewing HUD information in a vehicle. The questionnaire has six subscales, including Mental Demand (MD), Physical Demand (PD), Temporal Demand (TD), Own Performance (OP), Effort (EF), and Frustration Level (FL). The TAM is the most widely used model of user acceptance and technology use and is answered on a 7-point Likert scale. In our study, we used two subscales of the TAM: perceived usefulness (PU) and perceived ease of use (PEoU). The SUS consists of 10 items on a 5-point Likert scale and is a popular questionnaire for measuring the usability of a system. In addition to these three questionnaires, we used the System, a customized 7-point Likert scale questionnaire designed to investigate users’ perspectives on using the system, and the participants were interviewed after completing all tasks. The five questions of the System were as follows:
(1) AR Mapping: “It is easy to find out where virtual objects are located".
(2) Building Location: “It is easy to find out where a building is located".
(3) Display Size: “I am satisfied with the size of this display".
(4) Preference: “Overall, I am satisfied with this system". (5) Motion Sickness: “I feel dizzy".
5.3 Procedure
Upon arrival at the research lab for the experiment, participants were informed of the purpose of the research and signed an IRB-approved consent form. Participants received safety instructions and completed basic information about their age, gender, and AR experience. Before the experiment began, participants were given sufficient time to familiarize themselves with the system and to calibrate the position of the AR content displayed on the system to their eye level. The experiment was conducted on a university campus where the vehicle was under control and free from distractions such as traffic lights. The participant was seated in the right rear seat of the vehicle, and the participant’s head was kept as still as possible to avoid the effects of gaze shifts. Participants were instructed to keep their face toward the

right window in the FARS condition and toward the screen in front of them in the SBD condition, but we made sure that the view in SBD was as similar as possible to the view in FARS (we mounted a camera on the side of the vehicle to do this). During the experiments, the vehicle was driven at a constant speed (M = 15.1, SD = 1.2 km/h) along the designated experimental route (682.1 m) by a trained driver.
In the main experiment, participants attempted to remember the name and location of a fruit when an AR object with the name of a particular fruit was augmented on a building while traveling along an experimental path in each of the two conditions (FARS and SBD). Based on the method described in Fig. 3.2, the AR objects were augmented along the walls of the 3D building at locations closest to the vehicle. Upon arrival at the destination, the participant wrote the name of the fruit augmented by the AR object on the map at the location of the correct building. The participant then completed self-report questionnaires (NASA-TLX, TAM, SUS, System) based on the task. Participants were rewarded at the end of the experiment. The experiments were conducted in a within-subjects design, with the two conditions counterbalanced. All experiments were conducted in the early evening when traffic was light and light levels were constant. We conducted interviews after the experiments to obtain the participants’ opinions about the system.
5.4 Participants
A total of 16 participants (female = 6, 37.5%) participated in the experiment, with a mean age of 27 years, ranging from 23 to 34 years. 7 participants (44%) had previous experience with AR. All participants had normal or corrected-tonormal vision. All participants were college students and were recruited through on-campus advertising. All participants had walked the campus route but had never driven, so there was essentially no difference in their familiarity with the university environment. In addition, because the participants were already familiar with the route and the buildings, the experiment did not use the names of the campus buildings, but asked them to remember the names of the fruits attached to the buildings. In fact, when we interviewed the participants after the experiment, many of them said that it was very confusing to remember the names and locations of the fruits, regardless of their familiarity with the campus.
