6 Discussion
6.1  System performance
We extended the previous FHUD research (Wang et al. 2016, 2019) by proposing FARS for passengers in a real vehicle and performed empirical validation, which revealed some interesting findings. We developed a 3DCAR algorithm that creates in-vehicle AR content based on real external environments to validate usability. In addition, we implemented the system using a custom-made AR film because the content is not visible when projected directly onto a transparent screen (Coni et al. 2017). Most previous studies (Fu et al. 2013; Bolton et al. 2015; Gabbard et al. 2019; Zhang et al. 2021) have relied on simulations to conduct their experiments because it is difficult to accurately determine the position of the vehicle in real-world scenarios and accurately measure the distance from external objects to supplement AR objects. On the other hand, we implemented a FARS system in a real vehicle that is part of the environment and tested the system by driving the vehicle, demonstrating that AR-based infotainment systems can be implemented in real vehicles.
We considered three research questions and the corresponding hypotheses about the effect of different variables, and our results support most of the hypotheses. First, hypothesis 1 confirms that RTK has higher accuracy, similar to existing research (Lenain et al. 2003; Ling et al. 2019). In Study 1, we observed that the use of RTK results in a higher rendering frequency and lower values of update distance and position error compared to the use of GPS. As shown in Fig. 6, the distribution of points representing detected positions is significantly different for the two conditions, even with the naked eye. We observed that the AR objects stuttered and made it difficult to determine the position due to the inaccurate data under GPS. In particular, we found that the GPS conditions were navigated incorrectly and had bouncing data, causing objects to be intermittently rendered in the wrong locations on the display. These results show that when implementing an AR system in a vehicle traveling at high speed, using RTK instead of GPS can provide smoother rendering and higher system performance.
One of the most difficult technical challenges to consider during the design process is that the view of the environment through the side window can pass very quickly in the back seat or front passenger seat, making it difficult for the

system to identify and present external information (Charissis and Naef 2007). Previous studies that have implemented FHUDs in passenger cabins have not considered these difficulties and have used simulators to present information over pre-recorded video. FARS was designed to display information about the external environment for as long as possible while taking perspective into account, and the AR objects moved along the walls of the building based on the position of the vehicle. While our research was focused on passenger cars, the nature of the system could be more useful for public transportation, such as buses and trains. The system could display station information to passengers or show the vehicle’s route. It could also be used for location-based advertising, such as displaying information about nearby restaurants, cafes, etc.
6.2  Results of user study
The results of the spatial knowledge test showed that participants remembered the names of more objects in the FARS condition than in the SBD condition and were better at matching locations in the external environment with information about virtual objects. These results support previous studies and hypothesis 2 that have shown better results with systems using AR devices than with smartphones or paper maps (Rehman and Cao 2016; Chu et al. 2017). Existing studies have shown that augmenting landmark information with AR significantly reduces user workload (Bolton et al. 2015), and that information about places represented in AR provides users with positive feelings (Pamuru et al. 2021). Therefore, our results show that AR services using our system can be useful in landmark-centric advertising services that need to provide positive contexts in vehicles, or in training that requires memorizing information about roads and the environment.
In our study, participants reported that the visual comfort of AR provided by the large HUD and the perspective were the main benefits that increased their preference. In the interview results, most participants were satisfied with the large screen in the FARS condition and reported that the position of 3D objects was intuitively consistent with the external environment and easy to identify. While the SBD received a lower usability score than the FARS due to its limited display size, posture and viewing angle, the FARS can alleviate these problems due to its large screen and 3D depth. Therefore, we have shown that when implementing in-vehicle infotainment systems, it is important to properly map the external environment and virtual information considering the large screen and perspective to improve user preference. While current infotainment systems are typically configured with a limited size and fixed position, recent concepts

