introduced by car manufacturers apply higher levels of AR combined with vehicle automation (Wiegand et al. 2019).
Most questionnaire results showed that FARS is more useful than SBD, easier to use, preferred, and overall more satisfied with the system. These results supported our hypothesis 3 that the AR system provides a more positive travel experience than the HDD (Bengler et al. 2015; Berger et al. 2021; Wang et al. 2019). In the FARS condition, participants reported that they felt less cognitive load and were able to work more efficiently, and that they could easily identify the location of AR objects and buildings. These results suggest that FARS is a useful system for improving passenger experience and understanding of external environmental information, and contribute to the empirical evaluation of user satisfaction. FHUD in the passenger compartment can be applied in real driving environments, and is expected to increase user acceptance for various applications beyond navigation and vehicle information (Wu et al. 2009).
FARS gave the user a sense of perspective by varying the size of the virtual object according to the distance from the vehicle to the wall of the building on which it was projected. Participants reported that it was easier to map buildings and virtual objects in the FARS condition than in the SBD condition. In the FARS condition, participants looked through a glass window at a virtual object augmented on top of a real building, which allowed users to intuitively estimate the distance of the object from their vehicle and more easily map the object and the building in 3D.
In the SBD condition, some participants reported discomfort from constantly turning their heads to alternate between looking out the window and looking at the monitor screen. Five participants reported severe motion sickness in the SBD condition. Looking at the seatback monitor in a moving vehicle made them feel as if they were reading a book in a moving vehicle, whereas they reported less motion sickness in the FARS condition, which showed the wide external environment. These results suggest that in order to reduce motion sickness, a system design is needed that meets ecological validity by preventing the user’s visual focus from shifting between the real and virtual worlds (Abdi and Meddeb 2018).
To implement and use FARS in a real car, we need to consider several things: First, we need to use higher lumens to ensure that the AR content is clearly visible even in bright outdoor environments. Second, we need to make sure that the projector is well mounted on the car to prevent slight vibrations of the projected screen as the car moves. Third, we need a power supply that can provide enough power for both the projector and the PC. Fourth, we need to consider the cost of power, servers, and RTK devices to implement FARS.

6.3  Limitation & future works
This study has several limitations. First, there were problems with interference from other vehicles and pedestrians while driving on real roads. For Study 1, we only collected and analyzed data from trials where there was no interference from vehicles and pedestrians. For Study 2, we scheduled our trials at off-peak times, and there were very few situations where our vehicle came to a complete stop. Second, the vibrations of the vehicle were reflected on the screen due to the movement of the vehicle, so it is necessary to install the projector in a way that can absorb the vibrations of the vehicle as much as possible. Third, we examined differences between the two systems only in terms of short-term spatial cognition. It would be useful to conduct follow-up studies to investigate the effects of FARS on long-term spatial cognition and memory. Fourthly, for safety reasons, we only conducted the experiment on the university campus. However, maximizing the effectiveness of RTK is difficult in road environments where there are not enough RTK base stations or where tall buildings are densely packed. For these cases, we may consider allowing FARS to selectively use RTK or GPS. Finally, it is difficult to conduct experiments during daylight hours because sunlight interferes with the light from the projector. Thus, we were limited to conducting the experiment during daylight hours because the AR objects projected on the rear window screen were difficult to see in bright light. This is a common limitation of projectionbased AR using light. To overcome this limitation, we need a way to adjust the brightness of the projection according to the brightness of the current environment (Sato et al. 2006).
7 Conclusion
We built a Full-Window Augmented Reality System (FARS) for passengers in a vehicle and conducted experiments in a real vehicle to evaluate the system’s performance and user experience. The 3DCAR algorithm used in the FARS system makes it possible to create AR content that takes into account depth in real-world environments, and future researchers can further refine the algorithm and develop it into a form that can be used in real vehicles. The experimental results showed that for a windowed AR system in a fast-moving vehicle, where the alignment of real buildings and virtual objects is important, using a more accurate RTK sensor instead of a GPS with a high error rate can improve the accuracy of the data. The results of the user study empirically confirmed that the FARS system is more effective than the SeatBack Display (SBD) in enhancing human spatial knowledge and improving the user’s riding experience. Through these results, we have empirically confirmed that

